run('donald-trump', 'entities', 'May 2025 news cycle', """
In a *Meet the Press* interview aired in early May 2025, Trump discussed ongoing peace negotiations between Russia and Ukraine and how long he planned to give both countries before walking away from an agreement, saying "maybe it's not possible" to reach a deal.[N] Badlands contributor [[burning-bright|Burning Bright]] argued that Trump would be known as "the Peacemaker of our time" and predicted a total inversion of the post-WW2 Axis and Allies paradigm.[N]

During an Oval Office meeting with Canadian Prime Minister [[mark-carney|Mark Carney]], Trump announced that the United States would stop bombing the [[houthis|Houthis]] in Yemen after claiming the group had agreed to stop interrupting Middle East shipping lanes, an announcement made without advance notice to Israel.[N] He also teased a major forthcoming announcement tied to his upcoming Middle East trip.[N] Two days before the Carney meeting, Trump was asked by the host of *Meet the Press* whether he would consider using military force against Canada; he said only that he thought it was "highly unlikely."[N]

The [[supreme-court-of-the-united-states|Supreme Court]] allowed his administration to enforce the [[transgender-military-ban|ban on transgender people in the military]] while legal challenges proceeded.[N] His pick for U.S. attorney for the District of Columbia, [[ed-martin|Ed Martin]], suffered a likely fatal blow when Senator [[thom-tillis|Thom Tillis]] announced his opposition.[N] The hacker collective [[anonymous|Anonymous]] defaced the website of [[globalx|GlobalX]], the deportation carrier, calling Trump a "fascist."[N]
""")

run('scott-bessent', 'entities', 'Debt ceiling and China talks (May 2025)', """
Testifying before a House Appropriations subcommittee in May 2025, Bessent told lawmakers that a fresh estimate of the [[debt-ceiling-x-date|X-date]] was "forthcoming" and that the debt ceiling was already "on the warning track."[N] He explained that the Treasury was still counting tax receipts from the April 15 filing deadline and declined to give a hard figure, while assuring lawmakers the forecast would land soon.[N] "Just as an outfielder running for a fly ball, we are on the warning track," he said. "And when you're on the warning track, it means the wall's not far away."[N]

Bessent also said the United States would never default on its debt and that the debt ceiling would be raised, and stated that a [[central-bank-digital-currency|central bank digital currency]] is a sign of weakness that he would not support the Federal Reserve issuing.[N]

Ahead of the first major meeting between U.S. and Chinese officials since Trump imposed large tariffs on China — scheduled for that weekend in Switzerland — Bessent said he expected "de-escalation" to be the primary topic.[N] "We've agreed to talk – then on Saturday and Sunday, we will agree on what we're going to talk about," he told Fox News host Laura Ingraham, adding: "My sense is that this will be about de-escalation, not about the big trade deal. We've got to de-escalate before we can move forward."[N]
""")

run('mike-lawler', 'entities', 'IGO Anti-Boycott Act', """
Lawler introduced the [[igo-anti-boycott-act|IGO Anti-Boycott Act]], House Resolution 867, on January 31, 2025, together with New Jersey Democrat [[josh-gottheimer|Josh Gottheimer]].[N] The bill would expand a 2018 statute criminalizing participation in boycotts of Israel — which carries penalties of up to twenty years in prison and fines up to one million dollars — to cover international governmental organizations, and would permit prosecution of those who publicly shared information intending to connect individuals working for such IGOs with the Israeli or American governments.[N]

A vote scheduled for Monday in early May 2025 was cancelled after intense online pushback, including accusations from critics that Lawler was engaging in treason for endorsing the bill.[N] Badlands contributor [[ghostofbasedpatrickhenry|GhostofBasedPatrickHenry]] endorsed that criticism and called for the entire [[congress|Congress]] to be investigated for potential foreign influence.[N]
""")

run('ed-martin', 'entities', 'Collapse of the D.C. U.S. attorney nomination (May 2025)', """
Republican Senator [[thom-tillis|Thom Tillis]] said in May 2025 that he had told the [[white-house|White House]] he would oppose Martin's nomination to be the top federal prosecutor for the District of Columbia, a potentially fatal blow to his already dicey confirmation chances.[N] Tillis, who spoke to reporters after meeting with Martin the previous night, had previously signaled concerns over Martin's comments about the January 6, 2021 Capitol attack and his work defending participants in the riot, saying "I have no tolerance for anybody that entered the building on Jan. 6, and that's probably where most of the friction was."[N]

Senate Majority Leader [[john-thune|John Thune]] told reporters that Martin was now unlikely to emerge from the [[senate-judiciary-committee|Senate Judiciary Committee]], and Illinois Senator [[dick-durbin|Dick Durbin]], the panel's top Democrat, said that "in practical terms" the nomination was dead.[N] Badlands contributor The AlphaWarrior Show urged readers to make calls and apply political pressure in response.[N]
""")

run('thom-tillis', 'entities', 'Opposition to Ed Martin (2025)', """
In May 2025 Tillis said he had informed the [[white-house|White House]] that he would oppose [[ed-martin|Ed Martin]]'s nomination as U.S. attorney for the District of Columbia, a potentially fatal blow to Martin's confirmation prospects.[N] The North Carolinian, who was seeking reelection the following year, had previously signaled concerns about Martin's comments related to the January 6, 2021 Capitol attack and his work defending those who took part in the riot.[N] "I have no tolerance for anybody that entered the building on Jan. 6, and that's probably where most of the friction was," Tillis told reporters after meeting with Martin.[N]
""")

run('tren-de-aragua', 'entities', 'National Intelligence Council assessment (April 2025)', """
A newly released intelligence assessment found that some Venezuelan government officials "facilitated" the illegal migration of members of Tren de Aragua into the United States "to advance what they see as the Maduro regime's goal of destabilizing governments and undermining public safety."[N] However, the April memo from the [[national-intelligence-council|National Intelligence Council]], released by the [[office-of-the-director-of-national-intelligence|Office of the Director of National Intelligence]], stated that while Venezuela's "permissive environment enables TDA to operate," the [[nicolas-maduro|Maduro]] regime "probably is not systematically directing Venezuelan outflows, such as to sow chaos in receiving countries."[N] The memo waters down an earlier [[fbi|FBI]] assessment that the Maduro government was specifically directing TdA activities in the United States.[N]

Badlands contributor [[chris-paul|Chris Paul]] argued that the entire premise for the assertion that the Maduro regime assists Tren de Aragua rests on unverifiable sources according to the National Intelligence Council, and that those seeking to pin the gang on Maduro are supporters of a global regime long intent on removing him.[N] Commentary in the brief also noted the claim that Maduro was clearing out prisons and that some gang leaders escaped with assistance from the FBI and the [[drug-enforcement-administration|DEA]].[N]

Approximately 130 Venezuelan migrants alleged to be gang members were deported to El Salvador on March 15, 2025, aboard aircraft operated by [[globalx|GlobalX]]; a federal judge ruled on May 1 that the reasoning behind the deportations was illegal.[N]
""")

